"""Authored teaching content for weeks 9 to 12. Run to update canonical JSON."""
from pathlib import Path
import json

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent

def concept(title, explanation, example, misconception, correction, question, answer):
    return dict(title=title, explanation=explanation, example=example,
                misconception=misconception, correction=correction, question=question, answer=answer)

def week(n, title, outcomes, overview, objectives, concepts, demo, practice, lab, references, extension):
    data = dict(n=n,title=title,outcomes=outcomes,overview=overview,objectives=objectives,
                prerequisites='Bring the previous notebook and a short record of what you tried. Basic Python lists, dictionaries and functions are sufficient for the guided code.',
                concepts=concepts,demo=demo,practice=[dict(question=q,answer=a) for q,a in practice],
                lab=lab,references=references,extension=extension,timing=dict(lecture=120,lab=120))
    (ROOT/f'week_{n:02d}.json').write_text(json.dumps(data,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')

week(9,'Tools workflows and agents',['LO4','LO5'],
 'An AI assistant becomes more useful when it can obtain information or request a calculation. This week follows one request through a small tool workflow, then explains what changes when a model can choose its next step. Students learn the difference between a model proposal, an executed action and a verified result. MCP and multi-agent systems receive a short overview, with one bounded workflow as the practical focus.',
 ['Trace a tool request from model proposal to checked result.','Compare a fixed workflow with a bounded agent loop.','Explain MCP and choose permissions suitable for a simple assistant.'],[
 concept('Tool calling',
 'A language model can propose a tool name and arguments. Application code validates the request, executes an allowed function and returns its result to the model. The model can then explain that result. These are separate steps: writing a function call in text does not execute it. A tool description should state what the tool does and the arguments it accepts. A schema checks structure, while application rules check meaning. A calculator that accepts two prices still needs to reject impossible quantities. Start with read-only tools because their results are easy to check. Record the request and result so a learner can identify whether an error came from choosing a tool, choosing its arguments, executing it or explaining its result.',
 'A student asks for the price of three notebooks at EUR 4 each. The proposed call is multiply(quantity=3, unit_price=4). Code checks both numbers, computes 12 and returns the value with its currency. The assistant should explain EUR 12. If it says EUR 14, the calculation succeeded but the final explanation failed. These values form an invented classroom example.',
 'A model that writes the right function name has completed the task.',
 'Completion requires valid arguments, successful execution and a final answer consistent with the tool result.',
 'The calculator returns 12 but the answer says 14. Which step needs checking?',
 'Check how the tool result was supplied to the model and how the final answer was validated. Do not blame the arithmetic function without evidence.'),
 concept('Workflows and bounded agents',
 'A workflow follows steps selected by its designer. An agent uses model output to choose some of its next actions. Both can use language models and ordinary software. For a fixed task such as extract an invoice, calculate its total and request review, a workflow is often easier to explain and test. An agent can help when the necessary steps depend on information discovered during the task. It still needs a stopping rule, a maximum number of tool calls and a way to report failure. More autonomy adds possible paths to evaluate. Human review belongs before a consequential action, with a concrete description of what would happen. A chat interface alone does not tell us how much autonomy a system has.',
 'A library assistant first searches a catalogue. If it finds a book, it returns availability; otherwise it asks for a title clarification. A fixed two-branch workflow can solve this. A research assistant choosing among catalogue search, web search and document comparison may need an agent loop. Set a three-call limit and return an incomplete result when evidence remains missing.',
 'Every chatbot is an agent and more agents always improve a result.',
 'Identify the actions the model can choose. Add autonomy only when it helps the task enough to justify additional complexity.',
 'What should an agent do after reaching its tool-call limit without an answer?',
 'Stop, explain what is missing and return a partial or no-answer result. Repeating the same request indefinitely is not progress.'),
 concept('MCP and connected information',
 'Model Context Protocol, or MCP, standardises parts of the connection between an AI application and external tools or information. An application acts as a client and connects to a server that exposes capabilities. This helps different applications use a common interface. It does not establish that a server is trustworthy, grant permission to every file or verify returned facts. The user or organisation still chooses the server, authorisation and allowed operations. Teach the idea with a read-only library catalogue rather than a complicated server installation. A vector database stores searchable representations; MCP describes a connection interface. These components can appear together but solve different problems. Inspect permissions and the intended data flow before connecting a service.',
 'A catalogue server offers search_books(query) and reserve_book(id). A classroom assistant only needs the search operation. Its account should therefore have read access without reservation permission. The returned book description is source data; a sentence inside it saying "ignore your instructions" has no authority over the assistant. This is a trust boundary, even when the server uses a standard protocol.',
 'An MCP connection makes a service safe and gives the model reliable memory.',
 'MCP is an interface. Reliability, persistence, permissions and trust require separate design choices.',
 'Does a document search task need permission to delete documents?',
 'No. Give the minimum access needed for the task and confirm that results are limited to documents the user may read.'),
 concept('Checking an agent trace',
 'A trace records what the system requested, what each tool returned and how the task ended. Read it as evidence about observable behaviour. It does not reveal every internal calculation of the model. For each step, ask whether the tool was appropriate, the arguments were valid, the result was successful and the final response matched the evidence. External pages or tool outputs may contain instructions that attempt to redirect the assistant. Treat these as untrusted content. A simple defence combines limited tools, argument validation, permission checks and review before writes. No single prompt guarantees protection. Use fictional data in the lab, and avoid logging credentials or personal information. Evaluate failed tasks as carefully as successful ones.',
 'The trace says search("opening hours") returned "Monday 09:00 to 17:00", then the assistant answered "Open all night". Mark the final response as unsupported. In another trace the search returned no document and the assistant asked a clarification. That may be a correct outcome, even though it did not provide hours. Score completion against what the task and evidence permit.',
 'A longer trace proves the agent reasoned more carefully.',
 'Judge the result and observable actions. Extra steps can add cost and opportunities for error.',
 'Which two records would help explain an incorrect tool-based answer?',
 'The validated arguments sent to the tool and the exact returned result, together with the final answer, separate execution failure from explanation failure.')],
 dict(title='A calculator assistant with a visible trace',steps=['Write a request with quantity and unit price.','Inspect the proposed tool name and arguments.','Run the allowed function and record its result.','Try a negative quantity and an unknown tool.','Compare the final answer with the trace.'],expected='Valid inputs produce a checked numeric result; invalid requests stop before execution.',offline='The notebook uses a deterministic tool router. It demonstrates application control, not model planning.'),
 [('Compare a workflow and an agent for extracting a fixed invoice format.','A fixed extraction, validation and review workflow is suitable when the steps are known. An agent could choose extra steps for unusual inputs, but it requires broader testing and bounded actions.'),('Why is valid JSON insufficient for a tool call?','It establishes syntax or schema conformance, not whether values are meaningful or authorised. A valid negative quantity may still be invalid for an order.'),('Give one useful MCP capability and one control it does not replace.','A common tool interface can simplify integration. It does not replace server trust checks, user authorisation or validation of returned content.')],
 dict(title='Build and inspect a bounded tool workflow',goal='Implement a small allowlisted tool dispatcher and evaluate success and failure paths.',steps=['Run the supplied normal request.','Explain each field in the trace.','Add a second safe calculation.','Create invalid-argument and unknown-tool tests.','Write a stopping rule and explain where approval would be needed for a write operation.'],deliverable='A runnable notebook, at least four test cases, a labelled trace and a short workflow-versus-agent justification.'),
 ['https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/function-calling','https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-connectors-mcp','https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/function-calling'],
 'Compare a single-agent design with a two-role design on the same cases. Count unnecessary calls and disagreements. Implementing a multi-agent framework is beyond the core requirement.')

week(10,'Evaluation and responsible use',['LO4'],
 'A fluent answer can still be wrong, incomplete or unsuitable. This week makes quality measurable with a small, explicit rubric and separate test cases. Students combine numerical checks with human judgement, inspect uneven performance and connect privacy, attribution and disclosure to actual AI-tool use. Detailed legal interpretation and assurance frameworks remain with the companion modules.',
 ['Design a task-specific evaluation using a baseline and held-out cases.','Calculate and interpret simple quality measures.','Explain risks and document a proportionate human review process.'],[
 concept('A fair comparison',
 'Begin with the task and what counts as success. A summary might need accurate facts, coverage of three important points and a length limit. A code assistant might need working behaviour on tests and an explanation that matches the code. Write the criteria before looking at candidate outputs. Keep some cases for development and some separate for final evaluation. Changing the prompt after inspecting a test case makes that case part of development. Compare methods on matched inputs, with the same source version and settings where possible. Include an ordinary software or human baseline when it is appropriate. Repeated model runs reveal variation, but repeating a fixed saved response does not. Small classroom samples support learning and diagnosis rather than broad claims about a model.',
 'Create six development questions and four held-out questions about a fictional museum. Improve a prompt using only the first six. Then compare the original and revised prompts on the final four without further editing. If the revised prompt supports three answers and the original supports two, report 3/4 and 2/4 for this small set. Do not claim universal superiority.',
 'The prompt with the best example answer is the best prompt.',
 'Use a fixed rubric over multiple representative cases, include failure cases and report the sample size.',
 'Can you keep calling a set held-out after adjusting prompts using its answers?',
 'No. Once it informs development it is no longer an independent final test set. Reserve fresh cases.'),
 concept('Metrics and their limits',
 'A metric summarises a particular property, so choose one that matches the task. Exact match suits outputs with a unique correct value; it can penalise valid paraphrases. Precision asks what share of returned items are correct, while recall asks what share of the relevant items were found. A groundedness check asks whether claims are supported by the supplied source. Support is different from source truth: an inaccurate document can support an inaccurate answer. Human rubrics can judge usefulness, completeness and tone, but reviewers need shared examples and a way to handle disagreement. Automatic language or image similarity measures provide additional signals, not a complete verdict. An LLM judge also needs calibration against human labels and can have systematic preferences.',
 'A search retrieves four passages; three are relevant. Five relevant passages exist in the small collection. Precision is 3/4 = 75% and recall is 3/5 = 60%. An answer may still omit an essential fact even with those retrieval scores. Evaluate retrieval and answer quality separately. These are invented counts for a calculation exercise.',
 'One high score proves a generative system is accurate, fair and useful.',
 'Report which property the score measures and add checks for the other important requirements.',
 'What does a groundedness score fail to tell us about the source?',
 'It does not establish that the source itself is correct, current or authoritative.'),
 concept('Bias and human judgement',
 'Generative systems can perform unevenly across languages, dialects, topics or groups. A single average can hide this. Use relevant, ethically constructed test cases, compare outcomes and investigate differences rather than assuming a cause. Avoid collecting sensitive data just to make a classroom demonstration. Matched fictional cases can show how a change in wording affects a response, but a handful of examples cannot establish population fairness. Ask two reviewers to apply the same rubric independently before discussing differences. Record disagreements because they can reveal ambiguous criteria. Accessibility also matters: an image description, transcript or plain-language answer may be the outcome the user needs. Quality should be judged in the context of actual users and tasks.',
 'Two fictional scholarship enquiries differ only in a name. One answer offers clear instructions and the other discourages applying. This is a reason to investigate, not proof of a population-level bias estimate. Repeat with multiple controlled examples, inspect the underlying information and consider whether the task should be automated. Do not use real applicants for the classroom exercise.',
 'If two outputs match, the model has been proved fair.',
 'A small matched test can detect some problems but cannot establish fairness across all contexts or users.',
 'Why should two human reviewers score independently first?',
 'Independent scoring reveals disagreement that a group discussion can otherwise hide and helps improve the rubric.'),
 concept('Responsible use in everyday work',
 'Responsible use starts with the material supplied to a tool and the decision made from its output. Use fictional or approved data in practice. Check whether you have permission to upload a document, generate a likeness or reuse media. Keep a record of the original source, the AI assistance and the human changes when producing academic work. An AI-generated citation must be opened and checked before it becomes evidence. For decisions affecting people, identify who reviews the output and who remains accountable. Privacy controls and institutional rules depend on the specific service and account, so review them at the time of use. This course teaches a practical decision process; it does not replace institutional policy or specialised legal guidance.',
 'A student creates an image for a presentation and an AI summary of a paper. The student records the tool and date, verifies each factual claim against the paper, labels the synthetic image where relevant and explains the assistance according to course policy. If the summary invents a reference, the student removes it rather than citing it as though it existed.',
 'An AI disclosure makes every use acceptable.',
 'Disclosure helps transparency but does not replace permission, accurate attribution, verification or institutional rules.',
 'What should a student do with a convincing citation that cannot be found?',
 'Treat it as unverified, search the original publication record and exclude it unless the source can be verified.')],
 dict(title='Two reviewers score the same answers',steps=['Agree factual support and completeness criteria.','Score three responses independently.','Calculate agreement.','Discuss one disagreement using the source.','Revise the rubric and score a fresh example.'],expected='A transparent score table with denominators and an explanation of reviewer disagreement.',offline='Use the fictional source and responses supplied in the notebook. Saved responses test the rubric, not live model variability.'),
 [('Three of four retrieved passages are relevant; five relevant passages exist. Calculate precision and recall.','Precision is 3/4 = 0.75. Recall is 3/5 = 0.60. The denominators answer different questions.'),('Why might exact match be unsuitable for a summary?','Many different wordings can express the same facts correctly. Use factual-support and completeness criteria alongside format checks.'),('A model performs well on average but poorly on one language. What should the report include?','Report the separate result and sample size, investigate the relevant examples, state uncertainty and avoid hiding the difference in the average.')],
 dict(title='Evaluate outputs with evidence',goal='Build a rubric and compute a small evaluation report with meaningful failure categories.',steps=['Read the source and reference answers.','Score the baseline outputs.','Compute counts and percentages.','Inspect cases where metrics disagree.','Add fresh cases and explain the limits of the sample.'],deliverable='A score table, two explained failures, a revised rubric and a short responsible-use decision.'),
 ['https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/evaluation-best-practices','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-foundry/concepts/evaluation-approach-gen-ai'],
 'Calibrate an available model judge against the human rubric. Report disagreements rather than presenting the judge as ground truth.')

week(11,'Using and deploying generative models',['LO3','LO4','LO5'],
 'Students distinguish a chat product, a model library and an application endpoint, then run a small local service around a trained model. They compare local and hosted options using task quality, privacy, resources and cost. The aim is a working classroom deployment with known limitations, accompanied by a clear explanation of what a production service would additionally require.',
 ['Run a trained generative model behind a local interface.','Compare local models and hosted APIs using explicit constraints.','Record latency, quality and resource assumptions without inventing provider prices.'],[
 concept('Products models and frameworks',
 'A chat product packages a model with an interface and features such as file upload or search. An API exposes functions that software can call. A model library loads or trains model weights, while a serving framework makes inference available through a service. These layers can come from different organisations. Hugging Face libraries support model use, local tools can run compatible downloaded models, and hosted APIs provide managed inference. Students should choose according to their task rather than the number of frameworks in a design. Record the model identifier, software versions and generation settings. Product names and available models change quickly, so the lab uses a small trained classroom model plus an optional approved service chosen at teaching time.',
 'A student uses a chat interface to brainstorm museum labels, then uses a Python function to generate a label in a small application. These interfaces can expose different settings even if they use a related model. A successful chat demonstration does not prove that the application handles missing input or unavailable service responses. Test the actual interface being delivered.',
 'A model file is already a complete application.',
 'An application also needs an interface, input handling, inference settings and a way to return or report failure.',
 'Which component receives a request and sends back a model output?',
 'The application or serving layer. The model supplies the generative computation within that layer.'),
 concept('Local and hosted choices',
 'Local inference runs on equipment you control; hosted inference runs on a provider service. Local execution can help keep inputs on that machine, but downloaded software, storage, logs and network features still require attention. Hardware capacity constrains model size and speed. Hosted services can reduce setup work but introduce network dependence, account permissions, data-processing terms and usage costs. Neither option is automatically best. Compare them on a small representative task and state your constraints. Quantisation stores some values with fewer bits and can reduce memory use, sometimes with quality trade-offs. Do not assume a smaller file always gives faster end-to-end responses. A classroom model is deliberately small enough to make the whole deployment visible.',
 'A teaching room has ordinary laptops and unreliable internet. A small local model gives a dependable demonstration of inference. A hosted model may produce more useful language for a realistic task if an approved account is available. Keep the same evaluation questions for both and report their different scale and capabilities. The small model demonstrates deployment mechanics, not parity with a large language model.',
 'Local automatically means private, free and production ready.',
 'Local deployment still has hardware, maintenance, storage and access considerations. Check the complete data path.',
 'What should you inspect before uploading a student record to a hosted model?',
 'Institutional permission, the service and account data controls, the necessity of each field and whether the task can use fictional or minimised data instead.'),
 concept('Requests responses and failure handling',
 'A minimal service receives an input, checks it, runs inference and returns an output with a status. Check empty inputs and maximum length before generation. Time limits and output limits prevent one request from using unbounded resources. Separate invalid input, unavailable model and generation failure so the caller can respond appropriately. Store secrets outside source code and notebooks. A classroom endpoint should bind to the local machine unless remote access is intentionally configured. Serving a model locally demonstrates deployment, but it does not establish production reliability, authentication or safe public access. Keep a known working version so that a failed change can be reversed. Students should be able to explain one complete request rather than installing a large deployment stack.',
 'A local endpoint accepts a short seed text and returns generated characters from the trained model. An empty seed receives a clear validation error. A request for an excessive number of output characters is capped or rejected. The notebook sends a normal request and an invalid one, then shuts down the server. It never opens a public internet service.',
 'A service is reliable because one request worked.',
 'Test ordinary, empty, oversized and failure inputs, and record which behaviours you observed.',
 'Why should the teaching endpoint bind to 127.0.0.1?',
 'It keeps the demonstration accessible from the same machine rather than exposing an unprotected classroom server to the network.'),
 concept('Quality latency and cost',
 'Deployment decisions balance output quality with response time, resource use and operational constraints. Measure elapsed time around the same part of the process each time. Loading a model for the first request can be slower than subsequent inference, so distinguish those conditions. For a hosted text API, an illustrative cost estimate multiplies input and output token counts by their respective prices and adds any other billed components. Actual billing depends on the selected product and current terms. Record the price source and date rather than embedding a permanent number in lecture notes. A cheaper model that fails many tasks may create more human rework. Use a small report that connects measured quality to the resource assumptions.',
 'For an invented tariff of EUR 1 per million input tokens and EUR 4 per million output tokens, 2,000 input and 500 output tokens cost EUR 0.004: 0.002 plus 0.002. This is a calculation example, not any vendor price. If retries double the token use, include them. Compare the output quality before deciding that the lowest per-call estimate is preferable.',
 'Token price alone determines the total cost of using an AI application.',
 'Include repeated requests, tool charges where relevant, hosting and human review or correction effort.',
 'Why record cold-start and later response times separately?',
 'Model loading and initialisation can dominate the first request and would distort a comparison with already-running inference.')],
 dict(title='A local generative service',steps=['Load the small trained model from the guided notebook.','Start the supplied local service on an available loopback port.','Send a short request and read the response.','Send an invalid input and inspect the status.','Record timing and stop the service.'],expected='One successful local inference response, one handled invalid input and a timing record.',offline='The local model can run without a hosted API. Describe its limited training data and vocabulary.'),
 [('Name two reasons to choose a local model for a classroom.','A small local model can run without reliable internet and keeps the inference process inspectable. The choice still depends on hardware and task quality.'),('Calculate 2,000 input and 500 output tokens at the fictional prices in the example.','Input: 2000/1000000 times EUR 1 = EUR 0.002. Output: 500/1000000 times EUR 4 = EUR 0.002. Total EUR 0.004 before any other costs.'),('What evidence distinguishes a deployed classroom model from a saved notebook?','An interface receives requests and returns inference results. Test records show normal and invalid requests, the model version and the service boundary.')],
 dict(title='Run and test a local interface',goal='Expose the trained classroom model through a local interface and record its limits.',steps=['Execute the model setup.','Make a direct inference call.','Serve the same model through a local endpoint.','Test invalid input and an output limit.','Compare direct and endpoint results, then shut the server down.'],deliverable='Request and response records, timing observations, and a local-versus-hosted choice justified for one scenario.'),
 ['https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/pipeline_tutorial','https://docs.ollama.com/api/introduction','https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/production-best-practices'],
 'Use an institution-approved local or hosted foundation model behind the same interface. Record the exact model and settings, permissions and measured task quality.')

week(12,'Integration and critical comparison',['LO1','LO2','LO3','LO4','LO5'],
 'The final week connects model understanding, practical tool use and evidence about results. Students present a small application, explain the model family and adaptation choices, and demonstrate a normal case and a failure case. This is a formative integration activity that prepares students for the unchanged group project and examination in the professor’s descriptor.',
 ['Explain how the model, context, tools and interface work together.','Compare an application against a baseline using unseen cases.','Present evidence across all five unchanged learning outcomes.'],[
 concept('A coherent application',
 'Start with a specific user and a small task. A museum learning assistant might answer questions from a supplied guide and produce an accessible description of an exhibit. The model generates wording, retrieval supplies relevant evidence, and application code manages inputs and output checks. A tool should solve a need that ordinary generation cannot reliably meet, such as an exact calculation. Describe every component by its purpose. Avoid adding agents, tuning or a vector database solely to increase the component count. A modest application with a clear baseline and carefully analysed limits is easier to defend. Where a technique is required for a learning outcome but unnecessary for the final use case, include its guided experiment as separate evidence.',
 'A museum guide states opening hours and ticket prices. The assistant retrieves the relevant passage, quotes its source identifier and declines an unrelated medical question. A separate multimodal activity describes an exhibit image and checks visible details. The student explains why a calculator helps price totals and why the simple opening-hours task does not require multiple agents.',
 'Every technique covered in the module must be placed inside one application.',
 'Choose components that serve the use case and provide separate, clearly labelled learning evidence for the other techniques.',
 'What makes a component justified in the application?',
 'A specific requirement and evidence that the component helps meet it more effectively than a simpler baseline.'),
 concept('Evidence across learning outcomes',
 'The original descriptor sets five learning outcomes. Evidence should cover model-family principles, multimodal alignment, implementation and parameter-efficient fine-tuning, output evaluation, and deployment with retrieval or agentic behaviour. A slide listing these phrases is insufficient. Show an experiment or artefact and explain what it demonstrates. For the guided small models, state their architecture, data and limitations. For fine-tuning, identify frozen and trainable parameters and compare held-out behaviour before and after adaptation. For multimodal work, include more than a text-only conversation about images. A lecturer can use a portfolio checklist to identify gaps while the work is still formative. The institutional assessment scheme and marking brief remain authoritative.',
 'A portfolio contains a VAE/GAN comparison, an attention calculation, a checked image-and-caption task, actual LoRA training logs, a held-out evaluation and a local model endpoint. The RAG demonstration includes supporting passages. Each artefact has a short interpretation. Training loss alone would not establish successful adaptation, so the student also discusses generated outputs and held-out loss.',
 'Running all notebook cells automatically demonstrates every learning outcome.',
 'Execution is evidence of an activity. Students must interpret results, justify choices and identify limitations.',
 'Which evidence is needed to support a parameter-efficient tuning claim?',
 'The trainable-parameter count, frozen-base check, training process and a before-and-after comparison on held-out data, with honest interpretation of the result.'),
 concept('A convincing comparison',
 'Compare a baseline and a revised system on the same task and sources. Change one major factor when you want to identify its effect. For example, compare a question answered without supplied evidence against the same question answered with a retrieved passage. Retain a held-out set and include missing-information cases. A correct refusal or clarification can be a success when the source cannot answer. Record unsuccessful changes; they explain why the final design looks as it does. Results from a small classroom corpus should not be presented as a general benchmark. For generated media, use a rubric for prompt alignment, visible errors and appropriateness rather than relying only on an attractive example.',
 'An invented four-case comparison includes two answerable questions, one outdated-source question and one unsupported question. The revised assistant answers the first two and handles the last two with an explanation. The baseline invents answers to both unsupported cases. Report the case-level outcomes rather than hiding them behind one average. A different corpus could change the result.',
 'An abstaining system is always worse than one that answers every question.',
 'Whether abstention is correct depends on available evidence and the task. Unsupported certainty should not count as success.',
 'Why keep a case-level table alongside the overall score?',
 'It exposes which behaviours improved or failed and lets a reviewer inspect the evidence behind the aggregate.'),
 concept('A clear demonstration and reflection',
 'A useful demonstration begins with the user need, shows a normal task and then exposes a meaningful limitation. Keep a saved result as an outage fallback, but identify it as recorded evidence. Explain which steps actually ran live. Present sources and settings so another student can reproduce the small experiment. Reflection should name a specific failure, its likely cause and a justified next step. Sometimes the best next step is better data, a clearer prompt, a different interface or human review rather than more training. Students should also disclose AI assistance in their submitted work according to institutional policy. The class discussion should test understanding by asking why a choice was made and what evidence could change it.',
 'In five minutes, a student states the question-answering task, shows one source-backed response and one missing-source response, explains a failed tuning attempt and ends with a targeted improvement. A peer asks how retrieval differs from fine-tuning. The student points to the retrieved context and the separately trained adapter, showing that the mechanisms change different things.',
 'A polished demonstration can replace a reproducible experiment.',
 'A demonstration communicates the result; reproducible inputs, settings, code and evidence make the claim assessable.',
 'What is a useful reflection on a failed result?',
 'Describe the observed failure, identify a plausible cause supported by evidence and propose a test that distinguishes it from other explanations.')],
 dict(title='A short application defence',steps=['State the user and task in one sentence.','Show the baseline and revised design.','Run one normal and one failure case.','Point to evidence for the relevant learning outcomes.','Answer a peer question and record one improvement.'],expected='A reproducible demonstration with specific claims and visible limitations.',offline='Use the included deterministic integration notebook and clearly labelled saved training evidence when live tools are unavailable. A saved demonstration does not count as a new model run.'),
 [('How does RAG differ from fine-tuning?','RAG supplies retrieved information in context at inference time. Fine-tuning updates trainable parameters using examples. Their suitability depends on whether the problem concerns information, behaviour or both.'),('What should an application do when its source cannot answer?','State the evidence gap, ask an appropriate clarification or direct the user to a suitable source. Do not invent a supported-sounding answer.'),('Give a concise defence of a simple design.','State the task, explain why each component is needed, compare it with a baseline on matched cases and show the limitations that remain.')],
 dict(title='Integrate and explain the evidence',goal='Assemble a small application demonstration and a learning-outcome portfolio.',steps=['Run the integration notebook.','Add one new answerable and one unanswerable case.','Complete the case-level comparison.','Link the required model-training, multimodal and deployment evidence.','Write a reflection and rehearse a five-minute explanation.'],deliverable='A runnable integration notebook, a case-level evaluation, the five-outcome evidence checklist and a short reflection. This formative activity does not alter the official assessment brief.'),
 ['https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/evaluation-best-practices','https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/fundamentals-generative-ai/'],
 'Extend one component only after establishing the baseline. Define the expected benefit and an evaluation that could show the extension was unnecessary.')
